{"tool_name":"riboseed","contents":["biocontainers","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"riboseed","identifiers":["doi:10.1093/nar/gky212"],"license":"MIT","summary":"Riboseed: assemble across rdna regions.","home_url":"https://github.com/nickp60/riboSeed","keywords":["Base Sequence","Chromosome Mapping","DNA, Bacterial","DNA, Ribosomal","Escherichia coli","Genome, Bacterial","Genomics","High-Throughput Nucleotide Sequencing","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Sequence Analysis, DNA"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"riboSeed","home":"https://github.com/nickp60/riboSeed/","license":"MIT","summary":"Prokaryotic genome assembly polisher -- uses targeted subassembly to resolve gaps caused by repeated rDNA operons.","addition_date":"2019-10-29T14:41:43Z","last_update_date":"2020-06-16T10:55:26Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Workflow"],"version":["0.4.90"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:riboSeed","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"nickp60@gmail.com","name":"Nicholas Waters","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9035-2143","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9035-2143"},{"name":"Leighton Pritchard","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8392-2822","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["nickp60"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"cmd":"ribo run -r reference.fasta -F forward_reads.fq -R reverse_reads.fq -o results","input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence cluster (nucleic acid)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1246"}}],"note":"Choose an appropriate reference genome, (try out https://github.com/nickp60/plentyofbugs), add in some reads, and off you go","operation":[{"term":"Genome assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0525"},{"term":"Localised reassembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3183"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0925"}}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"riboSeed","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"nickp60","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gky212","metadata":{"abstract":"The vast majority of bacterial genome sequencing has been performed using Illumina short reads. Because of the inherent difficulty of resolving repeated regions with short reads alone, only \u223c10% of sequencing projects have resulted in a closed genome. The most common repeated regions are those coding for ribosomal operons (rDNAs), which occur in a bacterial genome between 1 and 15 times, and are typically used as sequence markers to classify and identify bacteria. Here, we exploit the genomic context in which rDNAs occur across taxa to improve assembly of these regions relative to de novo sequencing by using the conserved nature of rDNAs across taxa and the uniqueness of their flanking regions within a genome. We describe a method to construct targeted pseudocontigs generated by iteratively assembling reads that map to a reference genome's rDNAs. These pseudocontigs are then used to more accurately assemble the newly sequenced chromosome. We show that this method, implemented as riboSeed, correctly bridges across adjacent contigs in bacterial genome assembly and, when used in conjunction with other genome polishing tools, can assist in closure of a genome.","authors":[{"name":"Abram F."},{"name":"Brennan F."},{"name":"Holmes A."},{"name":"Pritchard L."},{"name":"Waters N.R."}],"citationCount":4,"date":"2018-06-20T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic acids research","title":"riboSeed: leveraging prokaryotic genomic architecture to assemble across ribosomal regions"},"pmcid":"PMC6009695","pmid":"29608703"}],"topic":[{"term":"Sequence assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0196"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"riboSeed","home":"https://bio.tools/riboSeed","license":"MIT","version":"0.4.90","summary":"Prokaryotic genome assembly polisher -- uses targeted subassembly to resolve gaps caused by repeated rDNA operons.","